<section class="vis-section" id="glossary" aria-label="Glossary and basics">
 <h2>Basics &amp; Key Terms</h2>
 <details class="vis-details">
   <summary>Retroreflective</summary>
   <p class="vis-small" style="margin-top: 8px;">Material that returns light back toward the source (e.g., car headlights + driver's eyes), appearing remarkably bright in-beam.</p>
 </details>
 <details class="vis-details">
   <summary>Fluorescent</summary>
   <p class="vis-small" style="margin-top: 8px;">Excellent for daytime visibility. It absorbs invisible UV light from the sun and emits it as visible light, creating a "glowing" effect. It does not glow at night under headlights.</p>
 </details>
 <details class="vis-details">
   <summary>Conspicuity</summary>
   <p class="vis-small" style="margin-top: 8px;">How much an object stands out from its background. Biomotion heavily increases conspicuity by revealing the human form and natural movement.</p>
 </details>
 <details class="vis-details">
   <summary>Observation &amp; entrance angles</summary>
   <p class="vis-small" style="margin-top: 8px;">Geometry matters: retroreflective shines brightest when the light source and observer are close. It sends it back near the source.</p>
 </details>
</section>

<section class="vis-section" id="studies" aria-label="Studies">
 <h2>Research Behind the Gear</h2>
 <p class="vis-small vis-muted" style="margin-bottom: 24px;">Every claim on this site links to published, peer-reviewed research. Key studies:</p>
 <div class="vis-resource-list">
   <div class="vis-resource">
     <strong style="color: #141515;">King, Szubski &amp; Tyrrell (2023), <em>Human Factors</em></strong>
     <p class="vis-small vis-muted" style="margin-bottom: 16px; margin-top: 8px;">Road users dramatically overestimate fluorescent brightness at night and underestimate retroreflective brightness, by a factor of 2× or more. One of the most cited studies on visibility misperception.</p>
     <a class="vis-btn vis-btn-secondary vis-small" href="/pages/visibility-illusion">Read: The Visibility Illusion</a>
   </div>
   <div class="vis-resource">
     <strong style="color: #141515;">Wood et al. (2012), <em>Accident Analysis &amp; Prevention</em></strong>
     <p class="vis-small vis-muted" style="margin-bottom: 16px; margin-top: 8px;">Closed-track study showing biomotion retroreflective configuration produces recognition at ~410 ft vs. ~55 ft for dark clothing and ~120 ft for a vest alone.</p>
     <a class="vis-btn vis-btn-secondary vis-small" href="/pages/biomotion-science">Read: Biomotion Science</a>
   </div>
   <div class="vis-resource">
     <strong style="color: #141515;">Tyrrell et al. (2016), <em>Clinical and Experimental Optometry</em></strong>
     <p class="vis-small vis-muted" style="margin-bottom: 16px; margin-top: 8px;">Comprehensive review of pedestrian conspicuity at night. Confirms biomotion placement and retroreflective material as the highest-impact interventions.</p>
     <a class="vis-btn vis-btn-secondary vis-small" href="/pages/retroreflective-vs-fluorescent">Read: Retroreflective vs. Fluorescent</a>
   </div>
 </div>
</section>

