<section class="vis-section" id="faqs" aria-label="FAQs">
 <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
 <details class="vis-details">
   <summary>Is fluorescent clothing enough at night?</summary>
   <p class="vis-small" style="margin-top: 8px;">No. Research shows that road users mistakenly believe fluorescent materials increase nighttime visibility. Fluorescent materials convert UV light from the sun into visible light, making them great for daytime. At night, without UV light, they go completely dark. You must use retroreflective materials to bounce car headlights back to the driver.</p>
 </details>
 <details class="vis-details">
   <summary>What should I prioritize if I'm on a budget?</summary>
   <p class="vis-small" style="margin-top: 8px;">The lowest cost entry to be seen is a pair of ankle and wrist bands. The next best step is building visibility directly into your normal gear by choosing <a href="/collections/reflective-gloves">reflective gloves</a> and <a href="/collections/socks">socks</a>.</p>
 </details>
 <details class="vis-details">
   <summary>How do I photograph a "before vs after" to test my gear?</summary>
   <p class="vis-small" style="margin-top: 8px;">Use a dark background and stand 10-15 feet away. Take one photo with your smartphone camera flash turned OFF, and one with the flash turned ON. The camera flash perfectly mimics the angle of a car headlight hitting the retroreflective material.</p>
 </details>
</section>

